<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Talents Around Us</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Who Will Perform in the Fiestas Patrias Activities?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Identify specific details in narratives that include expressions of feelings and attitudes.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify expressions of feelings and attitudes about participating in Fiestas Patrias celebrations (e.g., 'I'm proud to march in the parade.' 'I feel nervous about dancing in front of the whole school.').
Can recognize main ideas during group discussions about Fiestas Patrias preparations (e.g., 'Who will carry the Panamanian flag?' 'I will play the drums during the parade.').
Can understand specific details in conversations about parade roles and tasks (e.g., 'You need to wear your traditional uniform.' 'Don't forget to bring your instrument for the parade.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of pictures depicting various Fiestas Patrias activities and talents (e.g., a student playing the drums, a girl dancing folk dance, a band marching, a student waving the Panamanian flag). The teacher plays short sound clips related to these activities (e.g., drum roll, flute music, sounds of a marching band, crowd cheering). Students listen and point to the picture that matches the sound they hear, or say one word they recognize (e.g., "drums," "dance," "band"). The teacher asks, "What talents do you see for Fiestas Patrias?" or "Who can play the accordion?".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words from the theme using clear enunciation and visual aids (flashcards or picture dictionary): 'parade', 'band', 'drums', 'accordion', 'flute', 'talent', 'performance', 'excited', 'nervous'. The teacher also models short sentences using the future simple, 'can', and conditional phrases: "I will play the drums in the parade." "I can sing." "If you practice, you will perform beautifully." The teacher emphasizes minimal pairs in short vowels, such as /ʌ/ in 'fun' and /æ/ in 'band', by saying them clearly and having students listen and repeat chorally and individually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary and concepts.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 1: "Is 'excited' a happy feeling or a sad feeling?" (Expected answer: "Happy!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 2: "If I say 'drums' and 'hum,' do they rhyme?" (Expected answer: "Yes!") The teacher says other pairs like 'flag' and 'march' and asks, "Do 'flag' and 'march' rhyme?" (Expected answer: "No!").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">CCQ 3: "If you practice your dance, will you perform well?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Talents Around Us".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a short dialogue (approx. 1-1.5 minutes) between two 6th-grade students, Sofia and Mateo, discussing their plans and feelings for participating in the upcoming Fiestas Patrias activities. The dialogue includes expressions of ability ('I can play the flute'), future plans ('I will march with the flag'), feelings ('I feel a little nervous', 'I am so excited!'), and conditional statements ('If we practice a lot, our band will be amazing!'). It uses target vocabulary like 'parade', 'band', 'flute', 'drums', 'dance', 'performance', 'costume', 'talent', 'practice', 'celebrate', 'patriotic', 'colorful'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student identifies specific details and expressions of feelings/attitudes in short auditory narratives and discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> The teacher tells students to listen for the main idea. After listening, the teacher asks, "What are Sofia and Mateo talking about?" (Expected answer: "Fiestas Patrias plans and talents.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (Specific details):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False activity based on specific details from the dialogue. Example statements: "Sofia will play the drums." (False) "Mateo feels excited about his dance." (True) "If they practice, their performance will be amazing." (True) "Sofia can play the flute." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third listening (Feelings/Attitudes):</b> Students listen specifically for expressions of feelings and attitudes. They circle the correct adjective next to each character's name on their worksheet:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sofia: (excited / nervous / proud)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Mateo: (excited / nervous / proud)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice identifying specific details and expressions of feelings/attitudes, future plans using 'will', abilities using 'can', and possibilities with first conditional. Vocabulary focus: 'parade', 'band', 'drums', 'accordion', 'flute', 'talent', 'performance', 'excited', 'nervous', 'practice', 'celebrate', 'honor', 'traditional', 'patriotic'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Listen and Complete (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher plays short audio sentences one by one. Students listen and complete cloze sentences on their worksheet by filling in missing vocabulary words or grammar structures. Example: "I _______ play the accordion for the show." (can). "If I _______, I _______ perform well." (practice, will). "She feels _______ about the big performance." (excited/nervous).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Listen and Match (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> The teacher plays audio descriptions of students' talents and plans for Fiestas Patrias. Students listen and match each description to a corresponding picture (e.g., picture of a girl with a flute, boy playing drums, students dancing) or to a sentence stem expressing a feeling or ability. Example: "I will march in the parade carrying the flag." (Matches picture of flag carrier). "I can play a traditional song." (Matches sentence "He has musical talent.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Listen and Respond (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students receive a worksheet with pictures of different Fiestas Patrias activities and feelings. The teacher describes an activity or expresses a feeling (e.g., "The band will play amazing music!", "She is nervous about her dance!"). Students listen to the teacher and point to the correct picture on their worksheet. Then, one student describes an activity or feeling related to Fiestas Patrias using the target language (e.g., "I can play the drums." or "I am excited for the parade."), and their partner listens and points to the corresponding picture. They take turns. This activity reinforces listening comprehension and vocabulary recognition.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Talents Around Us".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Class Talent Show for Fiestas Patrias" - Students participate in a guided listening activity where they 'listen' to an announcement for a class talent show and fill in key details. The teacher acts as the 'announcer' or plays a pre-recorded audio of an imaginary school principal or teacher introducing upcoming Fiestas Patrias events, focusing on the class talent show and asking for volunteers. The audio will mention different roles and types of talents, incorporating target vocabulary, grammar, and expressions of feelings/attitudes. For example, "Good morning, students! For Fiestas Patrias, we will have a special talent show. We need students who can sing, dance, or play an instrument like the accordion or drums. If you want to perform, you will sign up after class. We are all very excited to see your amazing talents! Remember, if you practice, you will do great!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The teacher introduces the scenario: "Imagine our school is planning a talent show for Fiestas Patrias. Listen to the announcement and find out who can participate and how they feel."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Students receive a simple graphic organizer titled "Fiestas Patrias Talent Show Info." The categories are: "What will happen?", "Who can perform?", "What instruments or talents can they show?", "How do people feel?", and "What is important to do?".</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. The teacher plays the audio announcement (or reads it live) twice. Students listen carefully and fill in the information in their graphic organizers.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. After listening, students briefly share their answers in small groups, confirming what they heard. The teacher monitors for accurate listening comprehension.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the group sharing and collection of the completed "Fiestas Patrias Talent Show Info" graphic organizers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' ability to accurately fill in the graphic organizer based on the audio announcement. The teacher will check if students correctly identified the main ideas (what will happen) and specific details (who, what, how they feel, what to do) from the announcement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies main idea of the announcement (e.g., class talent show).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes specific talents/instruments mentioned (e.g., sing, dance, accordion, drums, flute).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Understands expressions of feelings/attitudes (e.g., excited).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Understands simple instructions/plans (e.g., sign up after class, practice).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. One new talent I heard about today is...</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. When people perform, they sometimes feel... (e.g., excited, nervous).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was the most interesting part of listening to people talk about Fiestas Patrias?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What words or phrases about talents or feelings did you understand best today?</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this listening lesson? The use of visual aids and varied listening tasks (gist, specific details, feelings) seemed effective. Students responded well to the audio dialogue and the talent show announcement. What adjustments are needed for the next lesson? Perhaps adding a simple drawing task during listening to cater to different learning styles. Ensuring clearer distinction between rhyming and non-rhyming words during pronunciation practice. I need to make sure students use polite language when collaborating.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will read a short text or story about students preparing for Fiestas Patrias, incorporating the same vocabulary and grammar structures heard today. They will read about different talents and feelings, building on their listening comprehension from this lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">2-3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>